    content: `Printmaking techniques are classified into four families by HOW the ink is held on the matrix (the block, plate, stone, or screen). (1) RELIEF — the ink sits on the RAISED surface. The artist cuts away everything that should print white, leaving the image standing proud; a roller inks only the high surface; light pressure transfers it. Woodcut, linocut, wood engraving, rubber stamps, and metal type (letterpress) are relief. (2) INTAGLIO (from Italian "to incise") — the ink is held in lines and textures cut or etched BELOW the surface. The whole plate is inked, then the flat surface is wiped clean so ink stays only in the recesses; damp paper and a very high-pressure roller press force the paper down into the grooves to lift the ink out. Engraving, etching, drypoint, aquatint, and mezzotint are intaglio, and they leave a "plate mark" embossed in the paper. (3) PLANOGRAPHIC — the image and non-image areas are on the SAME flat level, with no carving at all; they are separated chemically. Lithography (grease-attracts-ink, damp-blank-areas-repel-it) and its industrial descendant offset lithography are planographic. (4) STENCIL — the ink passes THROUGH openings. Screen printing (serigraphy) pushes ink with a squeegee through the unblocked mesh of a screen; hand-cut paper stencils (pochoir) and risograph work the same way. Many prints combine families (an etching with aquatint; a linocut over a lithographic background).`,
