    content: `A woodcut is the oldest form of printmaking (used for textiles and religious images in China and Europe centuries before movable type). It is a RELIEF process. The maker takes a block of fairly soft, even wood cut along the PLANK grain — a board sawn lengthwise from the trunk, so the long grain runs across the surface — and planes it smooth. A design is drawn on directly or transferred, always in REVERSE (mirror image), because the print flips left-to-right. Then, using knives (to outline) and V- and U-shaped gouges (to clear areas), the artist cuts away everything that should stay the colour of the paper, leaving the lines and shapes of the image standing up flush with the original surface. To print: a rubber roller ("brayer") lays a thin even film of stiff ink on the raised surface; a sheet of paper is placed on top; and the back of the paper is pressed against the block — by hand, rubbing with a smooth disc ("baren") or the bowl of a spoon, or in a press under light, even pressure (relief needs far less pressure than intaglio). The plank grain gives woodcut its characteristic bold, sometimes splintery, directional line. Japanese "ukiyo-e" prints (Hokusai's Great Wave, Hiroshige) are colour woodcuts, each colour cut on a separate block and printed in register; Dürer, and the German Expressionists (Kirchner, Nolde) exploited the raw carved quality.`,
