    content: `A linocut (lino print, linoleum block print) is a RELIEF technique — a direct variant of the woodcut in which the block is a piece of LINOLEUM instead of wood. Battleship or "artist's" lino is made of solidified linseed oil, pine rosin, cork dust, and pigment on a burlap backing (the same stuff as old floor covering), usually glued to hardboard or plywood for support, or supplied as a softer rubbery "easy-cut" block. Because lino has NO grain, it carves smoothly, cleanly, and with equal ease in every direction — long flowing curves that would splinter in wood come easily — and it is inexpensive, which is why it spread through art schools from the 1900s and was taken up by Picasso, Matisse, the Grosvenor School artists (Claude Flight), and countless printmakers since. Method is identical to woodcut: draw the reversed design, cut away everything that should print white with a knife and V/U gouges, roll ink onto the standing surface with a brayer, and print by hand burnishing with a baren/spoon or in a press with light even pressure. Multiple colours are made either with a separate block per colour (printed in register) or as a "reduction" print worked from a single progressively destroyed block.`,
