    content: `Wood engraving is a RELIEF process done on WOOD (not on a metal plate — that is a common misconception). The crucial difference from a woodcut is the surface used: wood engraving works on the END GRAIN — a block sawn ACROSS the trunk, so you are cutting into the exposed rings of the tree's cross-section — and it uses a very hard, dense, fine wood, almost always boxwood, with several small pieces bolted edge to edge to make a larger block. End grain has effectively no directional grain, so tools move equally in every direction. It is worked not with knives and gouges but with the fine steel tools of the METAL engraver — burins/gravers, spitstickers, tint tools, scorpers — pushed across the polished surface, which allows extraordinarily fine lines, cross-hatching, and stippling, and a full range of tone. Because the engraver cuts the lines that will NOT print, a wood engraving typically reads as delicate WHITE marks on a rich black field. It is still relief: the surface is inked with a roller and printed under light pressure. A WOODCUT, by contrast, uses the soft PLANK grain, knives and gouges, and produces a bolder, more graphic, more directional line. Thomas Bewick revived and perfected wood engraving around 1790; because a boxwood block is durable and could be locked up alongside metal type, it became the dominant method of illustrating books, magazines, and newspapers throughout the 19th century, until photomechanical processes replaced it.`,
