    content: `Etching is an INTAGLIO process in which acid, not a tool, does the cutting. Steps: (1) a polished metal plate (traditionally copper, also zinc or steel) is coated with a thin, even, acid-resistant "ground" — a mix of wax, bitumen/asphaltum, and rosin, either hard (applied as a solid and rolled thin, then often smoked) or soft. (2) The artist draws the image by scratching through the ground with an etching needle, exposing the bare metal along every line — this is easy and fluid, like drawing with a pencil, which is why etching lines look natural and gestural. (3) The plate is put in a tray of "mordant" — dilute nitric acid, or ferric (iron) chloride, or "Dutch mordant" — which corrodes ("bites") a groove into the metal only where it is exposed through the ground. Time in the acid controls depth: a line left in longer bites deeper, holds more ink, and prints darker and wider. The printmaker "stops out" (paints varnish over) the lines that are dark enough and returns the plate to the acid so remaining lines bite deeper — building a full range of line weights in successive baths. (4) The ground is cleaned off with solvent; the plate is inked so ink fills the bitten lines; the flat surface is wiped clean; and it is printed under great pressure on an intaglio (etching) press, forcing damp paper into the grooves. Variants: soft-ground etching (records pencil and texture impressions), spit-bite and open-bite, and aquatint (for tone). Rembrandt, Goya, Whistler, and Hopper are among the master etchers.`,
