    content: `Engraving, in printmaking, means LINE ENGRAVING: an INTAGLIO process in which the artist cuts the image DIRECTLY into a polished copper (or occasionally steel) plate, mechanically, using no acid at all. The tool is the BURIN (or "graver") — a short steel rod of square or lozenge cross-section, ground to a sharp diamond-shaped point and set into a rounded mushroom-shaped handle that fits the palm. The engraver pushes the burin forward through the surface of the metal at a shallow angle, and it ploughs out a clean, V-sectioned furrow, curling up a fine thread of metal ("spoil" or "swarf") ahead of it, which is trimmed away with a scraper. Line quality is controlled entirely by hand: pushing deeper and turning the plate makes a line swell and then taper to nothing, so engraved lines have a distinctive crisp, deliberate, tapering, controlled character — completely different from the loose, scratchy, even line of an etching needle. Burrs are removed so, unlike drypoint, the printed line is sharp. The plate is then inked, wiped, and printed on a high-pressure intaglio press. Because a well-cut copper plate can yield hundreds of good impressions and the line is so controlled, engraving was for centuries the standard method for reproducing paintings, and for banknotes, stamps, share certificates, maps, bookplates, and portraits. Dürer's greatest prints (Melencolia I, Knight, Death and the Devil, St Jerome in his Study) are engravings, as is most of the work of Schongauer, Marcantonio, and Hendrick Goltzius.`,
