    content: `Aquatint is an INTAGLIO technique for producing areas of TONE — flat washes of grey and black — rather than lines. (It has nothing to do with "Operation Aquatint," a 1942 British commando raid on the French coast.) A line-etched or engraved plate can only make marks by hatching; aquatint gives it the equivalent of ink wash or watercolour. Method: the plate is dusted with a fine, even layer of powdered ROSIN (pine resin), either from a "rosin box" or by hand-shaking through cloth, or sprayed on as an acrylic aerosol; it is then heated from below until the particles just melt and fuse to the metal as thousands of separate, closely spaced tiny mounds. When the plate is bitten in acid, the metal is protected under each rosin grain but corrodes in the gaps AROUND them, creating a dense, even, reticulated field of shallow ink-holding pits — which prints as a solid even tone. To make a range of tones: the artist "stops out" (paints acid-resist varnish over) the areas meant to stay lightest, then bites the plate a little; stops out the next-lightest, bites more; and so on, so each successive area gets a progressively deeper, darker bite. "Spit-bite" (brushing acid straight onto the rosined plate) gives painterly graded washes. Aquatint is nearly always combined with etched or drypoint lines for structure. Goya's series Los Caprichos, The Disasters of War, and Los Proverbios are the definitive aquatints; Mary Cassatt and Picasso used it extensively.`,
