    content: `Both terms describe prints that exist as unique images rather than an identical edition, but they differ in whether there is a reusable matrix. A MONOTYPE is a completely one-off print with NO permanent matrix. The artist works an image in printing ink or oil paint directly onto a smooth, unmarked printing surface — a sheet of plate glass, Plexiglas, or polished metal — either by painting it on ("additive" / dark-field) or by rolling the whole plate with ink and then wiping the image out of it ("subtractive" / light-field). A sheet of paper is laid on and run through a press (or hand-burnished), lifting essentially all the ink in a single impression. Because the "plate" carries no fixed lines, only what was just painted, a monotype cannot be repeated — a second pull gives only a faint "ghost" — so it is often called "the painterly print" and prized as a hybrid of painting and printmaking (Degas made hundreds). A MONOPRINT does have a fixed, repeatable matrix — an etched plate, an engraved block, a woodcut, a collagraph, a stencil — that carries a consistent underlying image which can be printed again and again. But each impression is then made unique by the way it is inked and finished: hand-colouring, selective wiping, à la poupée (dabbing several colours onto one plate), overprinting with monotype passages, chine-collé additions, or different paper. So a monoprint series shares a common structural image but no two are identical. Short version: monotype = no matrix, unrepeatable; monoprint = a matrix, but every pull deliberately varied.`,
