    content: `Two basic hand tools of RELIEF printmaking. A BRAYER is a hand roller for inking. It has a cylindrical roller — covered in rubber, polyurethane, or soft photo-gelatin — on an axle with a handle. You squeeze out a little stiff ink onto a flat, non-absorbent "inking slab" (glass or acrylic), then roll the brayer back and forth to spread the ink into a thin, smooth, even, tacky film (you hear it hiss when it's ready), and then roll that film onto the raised surface of your woodcut or linocut. Brayers come in different widths and different "durometers" (hardnesses): a hard roller lays a thin, crisp film and rides over carved areas; a soft roller pushes ink down into slight hollows and gives a heavier deposit. A BAREN is a burnishing tool for printing BY HAND without a press. It is a flat, roughly palm-sized disc with a smooth working face — traditionally a tight coil of twisted cord or bamboo fibre inside a backing, wrapped in a bamboo-sheath ("takenokawa") that is periodically replaced; modern versions use ball bearings or a hard plastic disc. After laying the paper on the inked block, you rub the baren firmly over the back of the paper in overlapping circular or figure-eight strokes, working from the centre out, which presses the paper into contact with the ink and transfers the image. It is the standard tool of Japanese "moku hanga" woodblock printing. (Neither tool is a "rolling bed" or anything to do with camera Bayer sensors.)`,
