    content: `In fine-art printmaking, an EDITION is a set of impressions pulled from the same matrix (plate, block, stone, screen), all inspected and approved as consistent with the artist's approved "bon à tirer" proof, then each one hand-signed and numbered by the artist, normally in pencil in the bottom margin (the fraction on one side, the signature on the other, the title sometimes in the centre). A "LIMITED EDITION" means the artist has declared in advance that only a fixed number will ever be made. READING "12/50": the number is written as a fraction — the top number is this particular impression's number, the bottom number is the total size of the edition. So "12/50" means "impression number 12 of an edition of 50." Two things it does NOT mean: (1) it is not the printing order — impressions are usually numbered after the whole edition is printed and shuffled, so #12 was not necessarily printed 12th; (2) it does not indicate quality or value — every impression in a properly editioned print is meant to be equivalent, so #3/50 and #47/50 are worth the same (a low number is not "better," despite what the market sometimes pretends; the only nuance is that with drypoint or mezzotint, where the burr wears, EARLY impressions genuinely are richer). Additional impressions outside the fraction exist as artist's proofs (A/P), printer's proofs (P/P), hors commerce (H/C), and BAT. When the stated number plus the sanctioned proofs have been printed, the edition is "closed" and the matrix is traditionally CANCELLED — scored, drilled, or defaced and then a "cancellation proof" pulled to prove it — so the edition can never be extended. An "open edition" is printed on demand with no set limit and is generally worth much less.`,
