    content: `A "state" in printmaking is a documented stage in the evolution of the matrix. As an artist develops an intaglio plate (or a woodblock, or a litho stone), they repeatedly stop, ink it up, and pull a working proof to see where the image stands. Any time they then ALTER the matrix — add etched or engraved lines, deepen a bite, lay an aquatint, scrape and burnish out an area, add or remove a figure, change the title or lettering — and print again, that new impression records a different "state" of the plate. States are numbered in order: "first state," "second state" (often written "i/iv," "ii/iv" meaning second of four known states), and so on, and for important printmakers they are meticulously catalogued in "catalogues raisonnés." Rembrandt is the classic case: some of his etchings exist in seven, ten, or more distinct states, and he sometimes printed and sold impressions of intermediate states as finished works; collectors pay enormous premiums for a rare early state. Whistler and Degas also reworked plates through many states. Distinguish "state" from two related terms: an EDITION is a run of many IDENTICAL impressions of the final, approved state; a PROOF is any impression pulled outside the edition (artist's proof, trial proof, state proof, printer's proof).`,
